            <ArticleTitle>The protective functions of omentin in cardiovascular diseases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>98-106</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cca.2015.05.019</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0009-8981(15)00282-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Adipose tissue is considered as a large gland that can produce paracrine and endocrine hormones. Growing evidence suggests that adipocytes may link obesity to cardiovascular diseases (CVD). Adipose tissue produces a large number of mediators, which affect metabolism, inflammation and coagulation. Omentin, a novel adipocytokine, has come into the center of interest due to its favorable effects on inflammation, glucose homeostasis and CVD. The present review provides a concise and general overview on the roles of omentin in CVD. The knowledge of these concepts may provide a new strategy to reduce disease risks on CVD in the future.</AbstractText>
